Certificate-Induced Knowledge Geometry:

A Riemannian Framework for Certified Scientific Discovery

Ouadi Maakoul

---

Chapter 1

Introduction and Motivation

Scientific knowledge, when accompanied by formal certificates of correctness, admits a natural differential‑geometric structure. The conventional practice of measuring similarity between models by a distance in parameter space is inadequate: two neural networks with nearly identical weights may possess radically different robustness guarantees, while structurally distinct controllers can be certificate‑indistinguishable. A meaningful geometry must therefore be induced by the certificates themselves, not by the arbitrary coordinates used to describe the models.

We construct such a geometry. Its core objects are

· the certificate space \mathcal{C}, a Riemannian manifold whose points are the mathematical objects that constitute a guarantee (Lyapunov matrices, barrier functions, robustness margins, etc.);
· the certified knowledge manifold \mathcal{K}, the set of all model–policy pairs that admit a valid certificate, together with a specific certificate, equipped with the pullback metric under the certificate‑extraction map \Phi;
· the knowledge potential \mathcal{U}(x) = U(\Phi(x)), a real‑valued function on \mathcal{K} that aggregates the certified utility;
· discovery dynamics, modelled as a Riemannian gradient flow on \mathcal{K}.

The principal contributions of this dissertation are:

1. A precise definition of \mathcal{K} and its pullback metric, with an explicit immersion condition, and a discussion of the smoothness assumptions required.
2. A complete analytic treatment of the double‑integrator stabilisation example, yielding a sharp geometric contrast: the intrinsic discovery curvature is not identically flat, but vanishes at the stability boundary, whereas the physical discovery curvature diverges as the reciprocal square of the distance to the boundary.
3. A conjectural characterisation of certificate walls and a proposal for modelling paradigm shifts as transitions between certificate atlases on a stratified manifold.
4. A summary that rigorously separates proved statements from conjectures and open problems.

The dissertation is self‑contained and theoretical; no numerical experiments are performed. The double‑integrator example serves as a minimal, fully checkable test bed for the entire geometric machinery.

1.1 Literature overview and positioning

The geometry of spaces of positive‑definite matrices is well established, principally through the works of Bhatia and the affine‑invariant metric of Pennec. Alternative metrics such as the Log‑Cholesky metric offer computational advantages and can serve as alternative certificate geometries. Riemannian metric learning provides the formal pullback machinery that we employ to lift metrics from certificate spaces to knowledge manifolds. The concept of pulling back a Riemannian metric through a feature map is a standard technique in manifold learning and has recently been exploited for generative autoencoders. Our innovation is to identify the feature map with the certificate‑extraction process and to study the consequences for the geometry of scientific knowledge.

The convergence of gradient flows on Hadamard spaces is treated in the monograph of Bačák, and we directly invoke those results. The synthesis and formal verification of certificates, especially neural Lyapunov and barrier functions, has seen tremendous recent progress; these operational techniques provide the concrete means by which the abstract space \mathcal{K} can be populated in practice. Finally, the stratification of spaces of musical chords provides the mathematical precedent for our stratified certificate atlases.

