Chapter 3

The Certified Knowledge Manifold \mathcal{K}

Let \mathcal{M} be a smooth manifold of models and \Pi a manifold of policies. A certificate for a pair (m,\pi) is an element c \in \mathcal{C} together with a proof that the property encoded by c holds.

Definition 3.1. The pre‑knowledge set is

\mathcal{K}_0 = \{ (m,\pi,c) \in \mathcal{M} \times \Pi \times \mathcal{C} \mid c \text{ is a valid certificate for } (m,\pi) \}.

We assume that \mathcal{K}_0 is a smooth embedded submanifold of \mathcal{M} \times \Pi \times \mathcal{C}; in concrete algebraic settings this can be verified directly.
The certificate‑extraction map is

\Phi: \mathcal{K}_0 \to \mathcal{C}, \qquad \Phi(m,\pi,c) = c.

Definition 3.2. The certified knowledge manifold is \mathcal{K} = \mathcal{K}_0 equipped with the pullback metric

g^{\mathcal{K}} = \Phi^* g^{\mathcal{C}},

where g^{\mathcal{C}} is the chosen Riemannian metric on \mathcal{C}. If the differential d\Phi_x is injective at every x \in \mathcal{K}, the pullback is non‑degenerate and \mathcal{K} is a genuine Riemannian manifold; \Phi is then called an immersion certificate map.

The geodesic distance between two certified triples is

d_{\mathcal{K}}(x,y) = \inf_{\gamma} \int_0^1 \sqrt{ g^{\mathcal{C}}(d\Phi(\dot\gamma), d\Phi(\dot\gamma)) } \, dt,

where the infimum is over all smooth paths \gamma in \mathcal{K} joining x and y. This distance measures the minimal certified behavioural change required to deform one certified model into another, as perceived through the lens of the certificate.

Lemma 3.3 (Invariance under reparameterisation).
If \Psi: \mathcal{M} \times \Pi \to \mathcal{M} \times \Pi is a diffeomorphism such that \Phi(m,\pi,c) = \Phi(\Psi(m,\pi),c) for all triples, then the pullback metric is unchanged. Thus the geometry depends solely on the certificates, not on the parameterisation of the models and policies.

Proof. The map \Psi induces a diffeomorphism of \mathcal{K} that preserves \Phi; since g^{\mathcal{K}} is defined solely through \Phi and g^{\mathcal{C}}, it is invariant. ∎

Remark 3.4 (Injectivity versus immersion).
In many situations the certificate map \Phi may be an immersion but not globally injective: two different model–policy pairs might produce exactly the same certificate. In such cases the pullback metric remains well defined, but the knowledge manifold may contain distinct points that are certificate‑indistinguishable. One can either quotient by the equivalence relation or restrict to a fundamental domain where \Phi is an embedding. The double‑integrator example below is a case where \Phi happens to be an embedding.

Smoothness of \mathcal{K}_0.
The assumption that \mathcal{K}_0 is a smooth manifold is non‑trivial. When the certificate condition is expressed by a set of algebraic equations or inequalities, one can often apply transversality theorems. For neural certificates verified by SMT solvers, the set is semialgebraic and may have boundaries and corners; a stratified treatment (Chapter 8) is then more appropriate.

