Chapter 4

The Knowledge Potential and Discovery Curvature

4.1 Certified utility

Let U: \mathcal{C} \to \mathbb{R} be a smooth utility function that quantifies the value of a certificate. The knowledge potential is the pullback

\mathcal{U}(x) = U(\Phi(x)), \qquad x \in \mathcal{K}.

For Lyapunov certificates we often take U(P) = \log\det P (the volume of the invariant ellipsoid). For scalar robustness margins one may use U(r) = \log r, and for multiple certificates a weighted sum is natural.

4.2 Riemannian gradient and Hessian

The Riemannian gradient of \mathcal{U} with respect to g^{\mathcal{K}} is

\operatorname{grad}_{\mathcal{K}} \mathcal{U} = (g^{\mathcal{K}})^{-1} d\mathcal{U}.

The Riemannian Hessian is the covariant derivative of the gradient,

\operatorname{Hess}\mathcal{U}(u,v) = g^{\mathcal{K}}(\nabla_u \operatorname{grad}\mathcal{U}, v).

Definition 4.1 (Discovery curvature – intrinsic and physical).
The intrinsic discovery curvature at x \in \mathcal{K} is the operator norm

\kappa_{\text{int}}(x) = \bigl\| \operatorname{Hess}_{g^{\mathcal{K}}}\mathcal{U}(x) \bigr\|_{g^{\mathcal{K}}}.

Researchers typically measure distances in a fixed parameter metric g^{\text{param}} (e.g., the Euclidean metric on policy parameters). The physical discovery curvature is

\kappa_{\text{phys}}(x) = \bigl\| \operatorname{Hess}_{g^{\text{param}}} \mathcal{U}(x) \bigr\|_{g^{\text{param}}},

where the Hessian is computed using the standard (Euclidean) derivatives in the coordinate chart. A large physical curvature indicates that an infinitesimal parameter change can cause a dramatic change in certified utility.

The distinction between these two curvatures is central. The intrinsic curvature reflects genuine geometric features of the knowledge landscape, whereas the physical curvature is an artefact of the chosen parameterisation and can diverge even when the intrinsic curvature remains regular.

