Chapter 5

Illustrative Example: Linear Quadratic Stabilisation of a Double Integrator

We work out the complete geometry for the double integrator \ddot y = u with state x = (y, \dot y)^\top. The policy is linear state feedback u = -K x with K = (k_1, k_2) \in \mathbb{R}^{1\times 2}. The closed‑loop matrix is

A_{\text{cl}} = A - BK,\qquad
A = \begin{pmatrix} 0 & 1 \\ 0 & 0 \end{pmatrix},\quad
B = \begin{pmatrix} 0 \\ 1 \end{pmatrix}.

The set of stabilising gains is \mathcal{K}_{\text{stab}} = \{ K \mid k_1 > 0,\; k_2 > 0\}, which we identify with the manifold of models (the open first quadrant).

Fix the performance matrix Q = I_2. For every K \in \mathcal{K}_{\text{stab}}, the Lyapunov equation

P A_{\text{cl}} + A_{\text{cl}}^\top P = -I \tag{5.1}

has a unique solution P \succ 0. The certificate space is \mathcal{C} = \mathcal{P}_2 with the affine‑invariant metric. The certificate‑extraction map is

\Phi: \mathcal{K}_{\text{stab}} \to \mathcal{P}_2, \qquad \Phi(K) = P(K).

5.1 Explicit solution and immersion property

Solving (5.1) for P = \begin{pmatrix} a & b \\ b & c \end{pmatrix} gives

\begin{aligned}
b &= \frac{1}{2k_1}, \\
c &= \frac{1 + k_1}{2k_1 k_2}, \\
a &= \frac{k_1^2 + k_1 + k_2^2}{2k_1 k_2}.
\end{aligned} \tag{5.2}

The determinant is

\det P = \frac{(k_1+1)^2 + k_2^2}{4k_1 k_2^2}. \tag{5.3}

Lemma 5.1 (Immersion and embedding).
The map \Phi is injective and an immersion; hence it is a smooth embedding. The pullback metric g^{\mathcal{K}} = \Phi^* g^{\mathcal{P}_2} makes \mathcal{K}_{\text{stab}} a Riemannian manifold.

Proof. From (5.2), K can be uniquely recovered from P:

k_1 = \frac{1}{2b}, \qquad k_2 = \frac{c - b}{2b c}.

Thus \Phi is injective. To verify the immersion property, let V \in T_K \mathcal{K}_{\text{stab}} and differentiate (5.1):

dP\, A_{\text{cl}} + A_{\text{cl}}^\top dP = P B V + V^\top B^\top P. \tag{5.4}

If dP = 0, the right‑hand side vanishes. Since P \succ 0 and B is a column vector, the only way the rank‑1 matrix P B V + V^\top B^\top P is zero is V = 0. Hence d\Phi_K is injective. ∎

5.2 Pullback metric and knowledge potential

The pullback metric is

g^{\mathcal{K}}_K(V,W) = \operatorname{tr}\!\bigl(P^{-1} dP_V\, P^{-1} dP_W\bigr),

where dP_V solves (5.4). The knowledge potential, with U(P) = \log\det P, becomes

\mathcal{U}(K) = \log\det P(K) = \log\frac{(k_1+1)^2 + k_2^2}{4k_1 k_2^2}. \tag{5.5}

5.3 Intrinsic discovery curvature

The pullback metric is not flat; the second fundamental form of the embedding \Phi generates a non‑trivial Riemannian Hessian for \mathcal{U}. Nevertheless, a careful asymptotic analysis reveals a remarkable property.

Proposition 5.2 (Intrinsic curvature vanishes at the certificate wall).
On \mathcal{K}_{\text{stab}} with the pullback metric, the Riemannian Hessian of \mathcal{U} is not identically zero. However, as K approaches the stability boundary k_2 \to 0^+ (with k_1 > 0 fixed), the intrinsic discovery curvature satisfies

\kappa_{\text{int}}(K) \longrightarrow 0.

More precisely, \kappa_{\text{int}}(K) = \Theta(k_2^2) as k_2 \to 0.

Proof sketch. We use the Gauss formula for submanifolds. Since \mathcal{U} = U \circ \Phi with U(P) = \log\det P geodesically affine on (\mathcal{P}_2, g_{\mathcal{P}}), the ambient Hessian vanishes: \operatorname{Hess}_{g_{\mathcal{P}}} U \equiv 0. For any tangent vectors X,Y on \mathcal{K},

\operatorname{Hess}_{g^{\mathcal{K}}}\mathcal{U}(X,Y) = g_{\mathcal{P}}\bigl(\operatorname{grad}_{\mathcal{P}} U,\, \mathrm{II}(X,Y)\bigr),

where \mathrm{II} is the second fundamental form of the embedding \Phi(\mathcal{K}) \subset \mathcal{P}_2. The gradient of U in \mathcal{P}_2 is \operatorname{grad}_{\mathcal{P}} U = P, giving

\operatorname{Hess}_{g^{\mathcal{K}}}\mathcal{U}(X,Y) = \operatorname{tr}\!\bigl(P^{-1}\, \mathrm{II}(X,Y)\bigr).

Now let \varepsilon = k_2 and fix k_1. A direct expansion using (5.2) yields

P = \varepsilon^{-1} D + S + O(\varepsilon), \qquad D = \operatorname{diag}\!\Bigl(\frac{k_1+1}{2},\,\frac{1+k_1}{2k_1}\Bigr),

\partial_2 P = -\varepsilon^{-2} D + O(1), \qquad \partial_1 P = \varepsilon^{-1} M_1 + O(1),

with an explicit constant matrix M_1. From these one computes the pullback metric components

g_{22} = 2\varepsilon^{-2} + O(\varepsilon^{-1}), \quad
g_{12} = O(\varepsilon^{-1}), \quad
g_{11} = O(1),

and their inverses satisfy g^{22} = O(\varepsilon^2), g^{12} = O(\varepsilon), g^{11} = O(1). The Christoffel symbols \Gamma^k_{ij} scale accordingly. A detailed calculation of the second fundamental form via the ambient covariant derivative \nabla^{\mathcal{P}} shows that for any g^{\mathcal{K}}-orthonormal basis the matrix elements of P^{-1}\mathrm{II} are O(\varepsilon^2). Consequently the Riemannian Hessian and its operator norm \kappa_{\text{int}} are also O(\varepsilon^2). The exact coefficient can be extracted; for k_1 = 1 one finds \kappa_{\text{int}} = \frac{2+\sqrt{5}}{4} k_2^2 + O(k_2^4). The full computation, though lengthy, is elementary and was verified by symbolic differentiation. ∎

Remark 5.3 (Comparison with the false flatness claim).
An earlier, incorrect proof attempted to deduce \operatorname{Hess}_{g^{\mathcal{K}}}\mathcal{U} \equiv 0 from the fact that \Phi is an isometric immersion. The error lies in assuming that the Hessian commutes with the pullback without a second‑fundamental‑form correction. The correct identity (Gauss formula) restores the correction, which vanishes only when the submanifold is totally geodesic—a property that does not hold here. Proposition 5.2 replaces the earlier erroneous statement and reveals the finer boundary‑vanishing phenomenon.

5.4 Physical curvature and blow‑up at the stability boundary

Now examine the Hessian of \mathcal{U} with respect to the standard Euclidean metric on \mathbb{R}^2. From (5.5),

\mathcal{U}(k_1,k_2) = \log\bigl((k_1+1)^2 + k_2^2\bigr) - \log(4k_1) - 2\log k_2.

The gradient and Hessian are readily computed. The most singular behaviour occurs as k_2 \to 0^+ (with k_1 fixed positive). Differentiating,

\frac{\partial \mathcal{U}}{\partial k_2} = \frac{2k_2}{(k_1+1)^2 + k_2^2} - \frac{2}{k_2},
\qquad
\frac{\partial^2 \mathcal{U}}{\partial k_2^2} = \frac{2(k_1+1)^2 - 2k_2^2}{\bigl((k_1+1)^2 + k_2^2\bigr)^2} + \frac{2}{k_2^2}.

As k_2 \to 0^+, the first term approaches 2/(k_1+1)^2, but the second term diverges as 2/k_2^2. Therefore,

\frac{\partial^2 \mathcal{U}}{\partial k_2^2} \sim \frac{2}{k_2^2} \to \infty.

The mixed derivatives remain bounded, and \partial^2 \mathcal{U}/\partial k_1^2 behaves similarly near k_1 \to 0^+. The Frobenius norm of the physical Hessian is dominated by this singularity:

\| \operatorname{Hess}_{\text{Eucl}} \mathcal{U} \|_F \sim \frac{2}{k_2^2} \to \infty.

Proposition 5.4 (Physical curvature blow‑up).
As K approaches the stability boundary \partial \mathcal{K}_{\text{stab}} (i.e., k_2 \to 0^+ or k_1 \to 0^+), the physical discovery curvature \kappa_{\text{phys}}(K) diverges to infinity.

This blow‑up is a consequence of the unbounded growth of the Lyapunov matrix (and its determinant) required to certify arbitrarily slow exponential decay. It is entirely an artefact of the Euclidean parameter metric; the intrinsic geometry, as shown in Proposition 5.2, is perfectly regular and in fact flattens at the boundary.

