Chapter 7

Certificate Walls and Generic Blow‑Up

A certificate wall is a boundary point of \mathcal{K} beyond which the current certificate type ceases to hold (e.g., a Lyapunov matrix fails to be positive‑definite, or a robustness margin becomes zero). As one approaches such a wall, the map \Phi typically ceases to be an immersion, and the pullback metric may degenerate. The double‑integrator example provides a precise illustration: the physical curvature diverges while the intrinsic curvature vanishes.

7.1 Conjecture on generic blow‑up

Conjecture 7.1 (Generic blow‑up at certificate walls).
Let \mathcal{K} be a certified knowledge manifold whose certificate condition is defined by the strict positivity of a scalar function \rho: \mathcal{K} \to \mathbb{R} (e.g., a smallest eigenvalue, a margin). Assume that \mathcal{K} is a smooth manifold with boundary \partial \mathcal{K} = \{ \rho = 0\}, that \Phi is an immersion on the interior, and that the utility U has a logarithmic singularity at the boundary, i.e. U(\Phi(x)) = \alpha \log\rho(x) + \text{smooth} with \alpha \neq 0. Then, for any smooth metric on the parameter space, the physical discovery curvature diverges as one approaches \partial \mathcal{K}.

The double integrator verifies this conjecture for the specific case \rho(K) = k_2 and U = \log\det P. A general proof would require formalising the certificate boundary as a stratified space and estimating the Hessian in the ambient parameter metric using transversality and singularity theory. The order of the singularity (logarithmic) is crucial; if U were bounded at the wall, blow‑up would not occur. Refining the conjecture to relate the blow‑up exponent to the order of degeneracy of the certificate condition is an interesting open direction.

