Chapter 9

Conclusion and Outlook

This dissertation has presented a rigorous Riemannian geometric framework for certified scientific knowledge. The certified knowledge manifold \mathcal{K}, equipped with the pullback metric from the certificate space \mathcal{C} and a utility‑derived potential, captures the notion that models should be compared through their guarantees, not their parameterisations. The double‑integrator example demonstrated the full machinery analytically, yielding a corrected and sharpened geometric picture: the intrinsic discovery curvature is not globally flat but vanishes at the stability boundary, while the physical curvature diverges at the same boundary. The physical blow‑up is an artefact of the parameter metric, not a genuine feature of the knowledge landscape.

9.1 Classification of results

· Proved statements: Lemma 3.3 (invariance under reparameterisation); Lemma 5.1 (immersion and embedding for the double integrator); Proposition 5.2 (intrinsic curvature vanishes at the certificate wall, scaling like \Theta(k_2^2)); Proposition 5.4 (physical curvature blow‑up); Proposition 6.1 (convergence on Hadamard manifolds, citing Bačák).
· Conjectures: Conjecture 7.1 (generic blow‑up at certificate walls).
· Definitions and framework: Certified knowledge manifold, pullback metric, knowledge potential, discovery curvature (intrinsic and physical), gradient flow dynamics.
· Open problems:
  1. Sufficient conditions for \mathcal{K}_0 to be a smooth manifold for broad certificate classes.
  2. Conditions under which \Phi is an immersion; methods to compute the pullback metric efficiently (e.g., via adjoint sensitivity analysis).
  3. Characterisation of the sectional curvature of \mathcal{K}; determining when it is Hadamard.
  4. Geodesic convexity of \mathcal{U} for non‑trivial certificates.
  5. Rigorous proof of Conjecture 7.1 using stratified Morse theory; generalisation to higher‑order singularities.
  6. Detailed geometry of stratified certificate atlases, including geodesics crossing strata and continuity of the knowledge potential.
  7. Algorithms for computing geodesics on \mathcal{K} and simulating discrete gradient flows, bridging the gap with Riemannian optimisation and metric learning.
  8. Quotient constructions for non‑injective certificate maps and the induced geometry on the space of certificate‑equivalence classes.

The present work lays a rigorous single‑stratum foundation upon which these stratified extensions and algorithmic developments can be built. It is hoped that the geometric perspective will illuminate the structure of certified knowledge and inspire new approaches to automated scientific discovery.

Appendix A

Riemannian Geometry Background

We recall essential definitions; for thorough treatments see do Carmo (1992) and Lee (2018).

Riemannian manifold. A smooth manifold M equipped with a smoothly varying inner product g_p on each tangent space T_pM is a Riemannian manifold (M,g).

Levi‑Civita connection. The unique torsion‑free connection \nabla compatible with g satisfies, for all vector fields X,Y,Z,

\nabla_XY-\nabla_YX=[X,Y],\qquad X\,g(Y,Z)=g(\nabla_XY,Z)+g(Y,\nabla_XZ).

Geodesics. A curve \gamma is a geodesic if \nabla_{\dot\gamma}\dot\gamma=0. The exponential map \exp_p:T_pM\to M sends v\mapsto\gamma_v(1).

Curvature. The Riemann curvature tensor is R(X,Y)Z=\nabla_X\nabla_YZ-\nabla_Y\nabla_XZ-\nabla_{[X,Y]}Z. Sectional curvature for a plane spanned by orthonormal u,v is K(u,v)=g(R(u,v)v,u). A Hadamard manifold is a simply connected, complete Riemannian manifold with K\le 0.

Pullback metric. If f:M\to N is a smooth immersion and (N,h) is Riemannian, the pullback metric on M is (f^*h)_p(u,v)=h_{f(p)}(df_pu,df_pv).

Gradient and Hessian. The gradient of a smooth function F:M\to\mathbb{R} is the vector field \operatorname{grad}F satisfying g(\operatorname{grad}F,X)=dF(X). The Riemannian Hessian is \operatorname{Hess}F(X,Y)=g(\nabla_X\operatorname{grad}F,Y). In local coordinates,

(\operatorname{grad}F)^i=g^{ij}\partial_jF,\qquad
\operatorname{Hess}F(\partial_i,\partial_j)=\partial_i\partial_jF-\Gamma_{ij}^k\partial_kF,

where \Gamma_{ij}^k are the Christoffel symbols of g.

Geodesic convexity. A function F is geodesically convex if for every geodesic \gamma, F\circ\gamma is convex. On a Hadamard manifold, a geodesically convex function has a unique minimiser, and the gradient flow \dot\gamma=-\operatorname{grad}F(\gamma) converges to it (Bačák, 2014).

