Appendix B

Geometry of the Positive‑Definite Cone \mathcal{P}_n

The set \mathcal{P}_n of n\times n real symmetric positive‑definite matrices is an open convex cone. With the affine‑invariant metric

g_P(X,Y)=\operatorname{tr}(P^{-1}XP^{-1}Y),\qquad X,Y\in T_P\mathcal{P}_n\cong\operatorname{Sym}(n),

it is a Hadamard manifold (Bhatia, 2007).

Geodesics. The geodesic joining P_0,P_1\in\mathcal{P}_n is

P(t)=P_0^{1/2}\bigl(P_0^{-1/2}P_1P_0^{-1/2}\bigr)^tP_0^{1/2},\qquad t\in[0,1],

and the distance is d(P_0,P_1)=\|\log(P_0^{-1/2}P_1P_0^{-1/2})\|_F.

Levi‑Civita connection. For vector fields X,Y (symmetric matrices) at P, the covariant derivative is

\nabla_XY = dY(X)-\tfrac12\bigl(XP^{-1}Y+YP^{-1}X\bigr). \tag{B.1}

Geodesic affinity of \log\det. The function U(P)=\log\det P satisfies dU_P(X)=\operatorname{tr}(P^{-1}X). Along a geodesic P(t)=P_0^{1/2}e^{tS}P_0^{1/2} with S=P_0^{-1/2}XP_0^{-1/2}, we have \log\det P(t)=\log\det P_0+t\operatorname{tr}(S). Thus U is geodesically affine and its Riemannian Hessian vanishes identically on \mathcal{P}_n.

