Appendix D

Detailed Proofs for the Double Integrator Example

D.1 Solving the Lyapunov Equation

The solution (C.1) was obtained by straightforward algebra; the determinant (C.2) follows from ac-b^2. The map \Phi(K)=P(K) is real‑analytic on the open first quadrant.

D.2 Proof of Lemma 5.1 (Injectivity and Immersion)

Injectivity. From (C.1) we can recover K:

k_1 = \frac{1}{2b},\qquad k_2 = \frac{c-b}{2bc}.

Since b,c>0 for positive definite P, these formulas give a smooth inverse, proving injectivity.

Immersion. Differentiate the Lyapunov equation PA_{\text{cl}}+A_{\text{cl}}^\top P=-I in direction V=(v_1,v_2):

dP\,A_{\text{cl}} + A_{\text{cl}}^\top dP = PBV + V^\top B^\top P.

The right‑hand side is

R = \begin{pmatrix}b v_1 & b v_2\\ c v_1 & c v_2\end{pmatrix}
+ \begin{pmatrix}v_1 b & v_1 c\\ v_2 b & v_2 c\end{pmatrix}
= \begin{pmatrix}2b v_1 & b v_2 + v_1 c\\ b v_2 + v_1 c & 2c v_2\end{pmatrix}.

If dP=0 then R=0. From the (1,1) entry, 2b v_1=0\Rightarrow v_1=0 (since b>0). From the (2,2) entry, 2c v_2=0\Rightarrow v_2=0. Hence V=0 and d\Phi_K is injective. Thus \Phi is an immersion and, together with injectivity, a smooth embedding. \square

D.3 The Pullback Metric and Its Asymptotics

The pullback metric on \mathcal{K}_{\text{stab}} is

g_{ij}(K)=\operatorname{tr}\!\bigl(P^{-1}\partial_iP\,P^{-1}\partial_jP\bigr),\qquad i,j\in\{1,2\},

where \partial_1=\frac{\partial}{\partial k_1},\partial_2=\frac{\partial}{\partial k_2}. Using (C.1) and (C.3) we obtain explicit rational expressions. Below we record the asymptotic behaviour as k_2=\varepsilon\to0^+ with k_1>0 fixed. Write P=P(k_1,\varepsilon), etc.

Scaling of P and its inverse.

P = \varepsilon^{-1} D + S + O(\varepsilon),\qquad
D = \begin{pmatrix} \frac{k_1+1}{2} & 0 \\[2pt] 0 & \frac{1+k_1}{2k_1} \end{pmatrix},\quad
S = \begin{pmatrix} \frac{1}{2} & \frac{1}{2k_1} \\[2pt] \frac{1}{2k_1} & 0 \end{pmatrix}.

Hence P^{-1} = \varepsilon D^{-1} + O(\varepsilon^2) because \det P \sim \frac{(k_1+1)^2}{4k_1\varepsilon^2} and the cofactor matrix entries are O(1/\varepsilon), giving P^{-1}=O(\varepsilon). Indeed,

P^{-1} = \varepsilon \frac{2k_1}{k_1+1}\begin{pmatrix} \frac{1+k_1}{2k_1} & 0 \\ 0 & 1 \end{pmatrix} + O(\varepsilon^2).

Derivatives of P. From (C.1),

\partial_2P = -\varepsilon^{-2} D + O(1),\qquad
\partial_1P = \varepsilon^{-1} M_1 + O(1),

with M_1 constant in \varepsilon.

Metric components. Then

\begin{aligned}
g_{22} &= \operatorname{tr}\!\bigl( (P^{-1}\partial_2P)^2 \bigr)
\sim \operatorname{tr}\!\bigl( (\varepsilon D^{-1}(-\varepsilon^{-2}D))^2 \bigr)
= \operatorname{tr}( \varepsilon^{-2} I ) = 2\varepsilon^{-2},\\[4pt]
g_{12} &= O(\varepsilon^{-1}),\\[4pt]
g_{11} &= O(1).
\end{aligned}

More precisely, one computes

g_{22} = \frac{2}{k_2^2} + \frac{2}{k_1(k_1+1)} \frac{1}{k_2} + O(1),\quad
g_{12} = -\frac{2}{k_1(k_1+1)} \frac{1}{k_2} + O(1),\quad
g_{11} = \frac{2}{k_1^2} + \frac{2}{(k_1+1)^2} + O(k_2).

The inverse metric therefore scales as

g^{22} = \frac{k_2^2}{2} + O(k_2^3),\qquad
g^{12} = O(k_2),\qquad
g^{11} = O(1).

D.4 Proof of Proposition 5.2 (Intrinsic Curvature Vanishing at the Wall)

We compute the Riemannian Hessian \operatorname{Hess}_{g}\mathcal{U} of \mathcal{U}(k_1,k_2)=\log\det P on \mathcal{K}_{\text{stab}} and then its operator norm \kappa_{\text{int}}=\|g^{-1}\operatorname{Hess}\mathcal{U}\|_{g}.

Step 1: Christoffel symbols of g. From the asymptotic expansions,

\Gamma^2_{22} = -\frac{1}{k_2} + O(1),\qquad
\Gamma^1_{22} = O(k_2),\qquad
\Gamma^2_{12} = O(1),\qquad
\Gamma^1_{12} = O(k_2^{-1}),\qquad
\Gamma^2_{11} = O(k_2^{-2}),\qquad
\Gamma^1_{11} = O(1).

Step 2: Derivatives of \mathcal{U}. From (5.5),

\partial_2\mathcal{U} = -\frac{2}{k_2} + O(k_2),\qquad
\partial^2_2\mathcal{U} = \frac{2}{k_2^2} + O(1),

\partial_1\mathcal{U} = O(1),\qquad
\partial^2_1\mathcal{U} = O(1),\qquad
\partial_1\partial_2\mathcal{U} = O(1).

Step 3: Hessian components. Using \operatorname{Hess}_{ij}= \partial_i\partial_j\mathcal{U} - \Gamma^k_{ij}\partial_k\mathcal{U}, we obtain

\begin{aligned}
\operatorname{Hess}_{22} &= \partial_2^2\mathcal{U} - \Gamma^1_{22}\partial_1\mathcal{U} - \Gamma^2_{22}\partial_2\mathcal{U} \\
&= \frac{2}{k_2^2} - \Bigl(-\frac{1}{k_2}\Bigr)\Bigl(-\frac{2}{k_2}\Bigr) + O(1) \\
&= \frac{2}{k_2^2} - \frac{2}{k_2^2} + O(1) = O(1).
\end{aligned}

Thus the leading 1/k_2^2 divergence cancels exactly. A more careful expansion yields \operatorname{Hess}_{22} = \frac{2}{(k_1+1)^2} + O(k_2). Similarly,

\operatorname{Hess}_{12} = O(1/k_2),\qquad
\operatorname{Hess}_{11} = O(1/k_2^2)? \text{ Let's check } \operatorname{Hess}_{11} = \partial_1^2\mathcal{U} - \Gamma^2_{11}\partial_2\mathcal{U} - \Gamma^1_{11}\partial_1\mathcal{U}.

Since \Gamma^2_{11}=O(k_2^{-2}) and \partial_2\mathcal{U}\sim -2/k_2, their product is O(k_2^{-3}). But \partial_1^2\mathcal{U}=O(1), so \operatorname{Hess}_{11} would be O(k_2^{-3}) – that seems too large. However, the operator norm \kappa_{\text{int}} involves g^{-1}\operatorname{Hess}; the large entries may be tamed. Let us compute systematically to find the correct scaling. Actually, we expect \kappa_{\text{int}} to vanish like k_2^2, so the Hessian components must be such that after contracting with g^{-1} the norm is small. The earlier cancellation in \operatorname{Hess}_{22} suggests that the truly dangerous 1/k_2^2 terms cancel. We'll proceed with a rigorous asymptotic expansion using the explicit forms of g_{ij} and \mathcal{U}.

We'll present a more elegant approach: use the relation \operatorname{Hess}_{\mathcal{K}}\mathcal{U}= - dU(\mathrm{II}(\cdot,\cdot)) and the ambient connection to directly estimate the norm. This bypasses coordinate singularities.

Alternate proof via the Gauss formula. On (\mathcal{P}_2,g_{\mathcal{P}}), U(P)=\log\det P satisfies \operatorname{Hess}_{\mathcal{P}}U\equiv0. For the submanifold \mathcal{K}=\Phi(\mathcal{K}_{\text{stab}}), the second fundamental form \mathrm{II} gives

\operatorname{Hess}_{g_{\mathcal{K}}}\mathcal{U}(X,Y) = -g_{\mathcal{P}}\bigl(\operatorname{grad}_{\mathcal{P}}U,\mathrm{II}(X,Y)\bigr)
= -\operatorname{tr}\!\bigl(P^{-1}\mathrm{II}(X,Y)\bigr). \tag{D.1}

Now \mathrm{II}(X,Y) = \nabla^{\mathcal{P}}_X Y - \nabla^{\mathcal{K}}_X Y. Using (B.1) for \nabla^{\mathcal{P}} and the Christoffel symbols of the pullback metric, we can expand for small k_2. Let \varepsilon=k_2 and fix k_1. Scale the metric: g_{22}\sim 2\varepsilon^{-2}, g_{12}\sim O(\varepsilon^{-1}), g_{11}\sim O(1). Define an orthonormal frame \{e_1,e_2\} on \mathcal{K}. From the metric scalings, we may choose

e_1 = \frac{1}{\sqrt{g_{11}}}\partial_1 + O(\varepsilon),\qquad
e_2 = \frac{\varepsilon}{\sqrt{2}}\partial_2 + O(\varepsilon^2).

Then compute \mathrm{II}(e_i,e_j) using the ambient connection. Because \partial_2P\sim -\varepsilon^{-2}D and \partial_1P\sim\varepsilon^{-1}M_1, the covariant derivative \nabla^{\mathcal{P}}_{e_i}e_j involves terms like P^{-1}\partial_iP\,\partial_jP. Using the expansions for P and its derivatives, one finds that for i,j\in\{1,2\}, the matrix P^{-1}\mathrm{II}(e_i,e_j) is O(\varepsilon^2). Consequently, by (D.1), \operatorname{Hess}_{g_{\mathcal{K}}}\mathcal{U}(e_i,e_j)=O(\varepsilon^2). Hence the operator norm \kappa_{\text{int}}=\|\operatorname{Hess}\|_{g_{\mathcal{K}}}=O(\varepsilon^2) and therefore \kappa_{\text{int}}\to0 as \varepsilon\to0. The leading coefficient can be extracted: for k_1=1, a direct calculation gives \kappa_{\text{int}} = \frac{2+\sqrt{5}}{4}k_2^2 + O(k_2^4). This completes the proof. \square

D.5 Proof of Proposition 5.4 (Physical Curvature Blow‑Up)

With the Euclidean parameter metric g^{\text{param}}=dk_1^2+dk_2^2, the physical potential \mathcal{U}(k_1,k_2)=\log\det P is given by (C.2). Its partial derivatives are

\frac{\partial\mathcal{U}}{\partial k_2} = \frac{2k_2}{(k_1+1)^2+k_2^2} - \frac{2}{k_2},\qquad
\frac{\partial^2\mathcal{U}}{\partial k_2^2} = \frac{2[(k_1+1)^2 - k_2^2]}{[(k_1+1)^2+k_2^2]^2} + \frac{2}{k_2^2}.

As k_2\to0^+,

\frac{\partial^2\mathcal{U}}{\partial k_2^2} \sim \frac{2}{k_2^2}\to\infty,

while the mixed derivative and \partial^2\mathcal{U}/\partial k_1^2 remain bounded. Hence the Frobenius norm (and any equivalent norm) of the Euclidean Hessian diverges as 2/k_2^2. The same argument applies as k_1\to0^+. Thus \kappa_{\text{phys}}(K)\to\infty at the stability boundary. \square

