Appendix E

Gradient Flow and Convergence

E.1 Gradient flow on a Riemannian manifold. For a smooth function F:M\to\mathbb{R}, the gradient flow is

\dot\gamma(t) = -\operatorname{grad}F(\gamma(t)).

It satisfies \frac{d}{dt}F(\gamma(t)) = -\|\operatorname{grad}F\|^2\le0.

E.2 Convergence on Hadamard manifolds (Bačák, 2014). If (M,g) is a Hadamard manifold and F is geodesically convex and bounded below, then the gradient flow converges to the unique global minimiser. The proof uses the fact that Hadamard manifolds are CAT(0) spaces and the flow is a curve of maximal slope.

E.3 Łojasiewicz inequality. Even without global convexity, if F is real‑analytic and \bar x is a critical point, there exist a neighbourhood U and constants c>0,\theta\in[0,1) such that

\|\operatorname{grad}F(x)\| \ge c\,|F(x)-F(\bar x)|^\theta\quad\forall x\in U.

Then the gradient flow with initial condition sufficiently close to \bar x converges to a critical point. This provides local convergence guarantees for many algebraic certificate conditions.

