Appendix F

Certificate Walls and Generic Blow‑Up

F.1 Formal Conjecture.
Conjecture (Generic blow‑up at certificate walls). Let \mathcal{K} be a smooth manifold with boundary \partial\mathcal{K}, and let \Phi:\mathcal{K}\to\mathcal{C} be a smooth immersion on the interior. Suppose the boundary is defined by \{\rho=0\} for a smooth scalar function \rho\ge0, and that the utility satisfies U(\Phi(x)) = \alpha\log\rho(x) + \text{smooth} near \partial\mathcal{K} with \alpha\neq0. Then for any smooth Riemannian metric on the parameter space, the physical discovery curvature \kappa_{\text{phys}} diverges as x\to\partial\mathcal{K}.

F.2 Supporting evidence. The double integrator verifies the conjecture with \rho(k_1,k_2)=k_2, \alpha=-2 (from \mathcal{U}=-2\log k_2+\text{smooth}), and indeed \kappa_{\text{phys}}\sim 2/k_2^2. The blow‑up arises from the second derivative of the logarithmic term, which contributes -\alpha/\rho^2 times gradient‑type factors. A general proof would use a tubular neighbourhood and the Morse lemma with parameters.

